  "Id": 1726,
  "Title": "New Faculty Publication: Earthen Architecture in Muslim Cultures ",
  "NewsSubTitle": "Historical and Anthropological Perspectives",
  "NewsSummary": "Professor Stéphane Pradines has published his edited volume \"Earthen Architecture in Muslim Cultures: Historical and Anthropological Perspectives\".",
  "GlobalNews": false,
  "AlternateNewsTitle": null,
  "AlternateNewsSubtitle": null,
  "AlternateNewsSummary": null,
  "NewsContent": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Professor Stéphane Pradines has recently published his edited volume "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Earthen Architecture in Muslim Cultures: Historical and Anthropological Perspectives ",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "italic"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "with Brill."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This edited volume follows the panel "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Earth in Islamic Architecture ",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "italic"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "organised for the World Congress for Middle Eastern Studies (WOCMES) in Ankara, on the 19th of August 2014. Earthen architecture is well-known among archaeologists and anthropologists whose work extends from Central Asia to Spain, including Africa. However, little collective attention has been paid to earthen architecture within Muslim cultures. "
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This book endeavours to share knowledge and methods of different disciplines such as history, anthropology, archaeology and architecture. Its objective is to establish a link between historical and archaeological studies given that Muslim cultures cannot be dissociated from social history. "
          },
          {
            "type": "hard_break"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "For purchase, please visit "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Brill website",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "link",
                "attrs": {
                  "href": "https://brill.com/abstract/title/35019",
                  "target": "_blank",
                  "linktype": "url",
                  "uuid": null,
                  "anchor": null,
                  "custom": {}
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
